Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.4. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (b) At any time prior to obtaining the Company Stockholder Approval, the Company Board may make a recommendation change <omitted> if, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors, the Company Board determines in good faith that an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The determination must consider not only the financial terms but also the strategic fit and long-term benefits for the Company. The Company Board must also evaluate the potential legal implications and fiduciary duties under applicable law. (c) The Company shall have provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to change its recommendation at least three Business Days before making such a change, detailing the reasons for the change and the terms of the Superior Proposal. During this period, the Company must negotiate in good faith with Parent to allow them to make adjustments to the existing agreement to match or exceed the terms of the Superior Proposal. (d) If the Company Board continues to determine, after considering any adjustments proposed by Parent, that the Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, it may proceed with the recommendation change, ensuring compliance with all applicable legal and fiduciary obligations. (Pages 45-46)\nAnswer:
