Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (f) The Company Board, in evaluating any Acquisition Proposal, shall consider the strategic benefits, operational synergies, and alignment with the Company's long-term goals, in addition to the financial terms. The Company Board must also consult with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors of national reputation to ensure compliance with fiduciary duties under applicable law. (g) The Company shall provide written notice to Parent of any intention to accept a Superior Proposal, and the Company Board must determine in good faith that the failure to accept such a proposal would be inconsistent with their fiduciary duties. (h) In the event of any amendment to the strategic or operational terms of an Acquisition Proposal, the Company shall issue a new notice to Parent and enter a new negotiation period, ensuring that all aspects, including non-financial, are considered. (Pages 88-89)
