Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Offers. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board shall evaluate any Acquisition Proposal by considering, in good faith, the strategic alignment of the proposal with the Company's long-term goals, in addition to financial terms. The Company Board, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, must determine that the proposal does not conflict with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law. Furthermore, the Company Board shall give due consideration to the social and economic impact of the proposal on the Company's stakeholders, including employees and customers, and shall negotiate in good faith with the proposing party to address any concerns. (g) The Company Board shall provide the Parent with written notice of any intention to recommend a proposal as superior, outlining the strategic benefits and potential synergies identified. Such notice must be given at least five Business Days prior to making any recommendation change. The Company Board shall engage in discussions with Parent during this period to explore potential amendments to the existing agreement to align with the Company's strategic objectives. (Pages 102-103)\nAnswer:
